---
// Core page. Copy authored against briefs/toy-teacup-maltipoo.json (2026-07-13).
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PawTrail from '../components/PawTrail.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AnswerCapsule from '../components/AnswerCapsule.astro';
import CaptionedBlock from '../components/CaptionedBlock.astro';
import FactsCard from '../components/FactsCard.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CtaClose from '../components/CtaClose.astro';
import { whatsappLink } from '../data/site';
import { webPageSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';
import featuredImg from '../assets/site/14-maltipoo-toy-hands.webp';

const title = 'Toy, Teacup and Mini Maltipoos, Honestly | Maltipoo.sg';
const description =
  'Teacup Maltipoo, toy Maltipoo, mini: what the labels mean, what is marketing, the health risks of extreme miniaturisation.';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Toy & Teacup', url: '/toy-teacup-maltipoo' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Are teacup Maltipoos real?',
    a: 'As a marketing label, everywhere; as a registered size class, nowhere. No kennel or breed body defines "teacup", and a teacup maltipoo full grown usually lands right in normal toy territory. It typically means an unusually small puppy sold at a premium, often just a normal toy photographed young.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How small is too small?',
    a: 'Vets flag adult weights much under 2kg as elevated-risk territory for hypoglycemia, fragile bones and dental crowding. A healthy toy at 2 to 4kg is already among the smallest dogs in any lift lobby.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do teacup listings cost so much more?',
    a: 'Because the word sells. The premium buys a label, not a healthier or more finished dog, and sometimes it buys miniaturisation the puppy pays for later. Our price drivers are colour and size within the healthy toy range, nothing else.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a shop guarantee adult size?',
    a: 'No honest one can. The parents are the best predictor, which is why we keep their sizing on record and show you in person. Anyone guaranteeing an exact adult weight is guessing out loud.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will a toy Maltipoo fit standard carriers?',
    a: 'Comfortably. At 2 to 4kg it fits soft carriers approved for MRT and buses and cabin-size airline carriers, one of the practical perks of the true toy size.',
  },
];

const schemas = [
  webPageSchema({
    path: '/toy-teacup-maltipoo',
    title,
    description,
    aboutName: 'Toy, teacup and mini Maltipoo sizes',
    datePublished: '2026-07-13',
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  faqSchema(faqItems),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/toy-teacup-maltipoo" schemas={schemas}>
  <section class="container-site pt-4">
    <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-panel)] bg-tint-200 px-6 py-10 md:px-12 relative overflow-hidden rise-in">
        <div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="absolute -top-8 -right-8 w-44 h-44 text-white/40" viewBox="0 0 100 100" fill="currentColor"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="50" /></svg>
          <svg class="absolute top-6 right-[22%] w-9 h-9 text-primary/25 float-soft" style="--float-rot: 14deg" viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="currentColor"><circle cx="16" cy="19" r="9" /><circle cx="8" cy="11" r="3.4" /><circle cx="16" cy="8.5" r="3.4" /><circle cx="24" cy="11" r="3.4" /></svg>
          <svg class="absolute bottom-5 right-[10%] w-7 h-7 text-primary/20 float-soft" style="--float-rot: -10deg; animation-delay: -2.6s" viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="currentColor"><circle cx="16" cy="19" r="9" /><circle cx="8" cy="11" r="3.4" /><circle cx="16" cy="8.5" r="3.4" /><circle cx="24" cy="11" r="3.4" /></svg>
          <svg class="absolute -bottom-10 -left-10 w-40 h-40 text-primary/8" viewBox="0 0 100 100" fill="currentColor"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="50" /></svg>
          <div class="absolute bottom-2 left-0 right-0 opacity-30"><PawTrail /></div>
        </div>
        <div class="relative">
      <Breadcrumb items={crumbs} bare class="mb-5" />
      <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Sizes & labels</p>
      <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.9rem,4vw,2.8rem)] mb-3 max-w-[26ch]">Toy, teacup and mini Maltipoos, honestly explained</h1>
      <p class="text-ink-soft max-w-[52ch] mb-5">
        One of these words describes a dog; one describes a Poodle parent; one describes a
        price strategy. Sorting them out saves you thousands and the puppy a lot more.
      </p>
      <a href={whatsappLink('Hi! Could you explain the sizes of your available Maltipoo puppies?')} class="btn-cta">Ask about real sizes</a>
    </div></div>
  </section>

  <AnswerCapsule question="What is the difference between toy, teacup and mini Maltipoos?">
    Toy and mini describe the Poodle parent: a toy maltipoo finishes 2 to 4kg, a mini
    maltipoo 4 to 6kg. Teacup is a marketing label with no registered standard anywhere,
    usually meaning an unusually small puppy at a premium, with health risks attached. We
    place healthy toys and skip the label.
  </AnswerCapsule>

  <section class="container-site pt-8">
    <img
      src={featuredImg.src}
      width={featuredImg.width}
      height={featuredImg.height}
      alt="Tiny toy Maltipoo puppy held gently in two hands showing its small size"
      loading="lazy"
      class="rounded-[var(--radius-media)] w-full h-auto shadow-[var(--shadow-pop)]"
    />
  </section>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="What the labels actually map to" id="labels">
    <p>
      Toy and mini are real, boring words: they name which Poodle was the parent, and they
      predict the adult ranges in the <a href="/maltipoo-size">size guide</a>. Teacup maps
      to nothing. No kennel club, no breed registry, no veterinary body defines it, which is
      why a "micro teacup maltipoo" import can be marketed here at any weight the seller
      likes.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will meet the label constantly in the "teacup maltipoo Singapore" search results, teacup maltipoo price threads included, because it converts. That is its entire job.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Label decoder"
      items={[
        'Toy: Toy Poodle parent, 2 to 4kg adult',
        'Mini: Miniature Poodle parent, 4 to 6kg adult',
        'Teacup: no standard, anywhere, full stop',
        'Micro teacup: the same word, twice the markup',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="The health cost of extreme small" id="health" reverse>
    <p>
      Push a toy breed smaller than nature keeps it and predictable bills follow:
      hypoglycemia crashes in puppies too small to buffer a missed meal, bones that snap
      from sofa-height jumps, and dental crowding in a jaw with no room to argue. Some of the smallest are simply runts
      relabelled at a premium.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this is the puppy's fault, and none of it is visible in the photo. It is why
      the <a href="/maltipoo-lifespan-health">health guide</a> talks about weight as the
      first longevity lever, and why our floor is a healthy toy, not the smallest sellable
      dog.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Below-2kg risk list"
      items={[
        'Hypoglycemia from missed meals',
        'Fragile bones, sofa-jump fractures',
        'Dental crowding in a tiny jaw',
        'Runt-relabelling at a premium',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="The economics of the label" id="economics" tinted>
    <p>
      A note from the shop floor: buyers regularly arrive with a "teacup budget" a thousand
      dollars above our range, built from listings where the word did the pricing. Within
      our {`$3,288 to $5,988`} all-in range, the <a href="/maltipoo-price-singapore">price
      guide</a> is explicit: colour and size within the healthy toy range move the figure,
      and no word on a tag ever does.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Price sanity check"
      items={[
        'Premium for the word teacup: $0 here',
        'Real drivers: colour, healthy-range size',
        'A label is not a warranty',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="What we place instead" id="instead">
    <p>
      The maltipoo toy size is the sweet spot: healthy toy Maltipoos, 2 to 4kg full grown, parents shown so you can see the finished
      size standing in front of you. At that weight the dog is already featherweight for any
      flat, any carrier and any lap; smaller buys risk, not convenience.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Our line"
      items={[
        'Healthy toys, 2 to 4kg, parents on record',
        'No teacup label, on cards or in chat',
        'Already the smallest dog in most lifts',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Living tiny in Singapore: transport, furniture, physics" id="tiny-living">
    <p>
      A 2 to 4kg toy Maltipoo is the easiest dog in Singapore to move: it fits every standard airline-style carrier, rides GrabPet without drama, and slips into cafes that would blink at a Cockapoo. Public trains and buses stay off-limits to pet dogs regardless of size, so the carrier-plus-Grab routine in our <a href="/blog/maltipoo-transport-singapore">transport guide</a> is the real playbook.
    </p>
    <p>
      The physics cut the other way at home. A sofa jump that a 10kg dog shrugs off is a fracture risk at 2.5kg, so pet steps beside the bed and a no-launch house rule are day-one equipment, not accessories. The same maths powers the with-kids rules: floor-level play, no carrying while walking, and the <a href="/blog/maltipoo-with-kids">house rules</a> on the fridge.
    </p>
    <p>
      Feeding tiny is its own discipline: small stomachs and quick metabolisms mean measured meals on schedule, and a puppy that skips two meals is a same-day call, not a wait-and-see. The <a href="/blog/maltipoo-food">feeding guide</a> carries the portions.
    </p>
    <p>
      All of this is why our floor stays toy-first and teacup-never: the toy band keeps the convenience of small without buying the fragility of extreme small. That line is the whole page in one sentence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Grooming tiny has one quirk worth knowing: small dogs lose heat fast when wet, so home baths are short, warm and followed by a proper blow-dry, never a damp air-dry under aircon. Salons handle toy breeds daily, but say the weight out loud when booking; a groomer who plans for 2.5kg schedules a gentler table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Socialisation also scales down, in venue rather than in importance. A tiny puppy overwhelmed at a dog run learns fear, not friendship; padded-lap introductions, calm adult dogs and carrier-level city exposure through the vaccination window build the same confidence at toy scale. The <a href="/maltipoo-training">training guide</a> carries the schedule.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Tiny-dog day one"
      items={[
        "Fits all standard carriers; GrabPet easy",
        "MRT/buses: no pet dogs, any size",
        "Pet steps + no-launch rule from day one",
        "Two skipped meals = same-day call",
        "Toy band: small without fragile"
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />

  <CtaClose
    secondaryHeading="See real toy sizes"
    secondaryText="The available puppies are all healthy-range toys; ask for the parents' sizing."
    secondaryHref="/puppies"
    secondaryLabel="See available puppies"
  />
</BaseLayout>
